<p>We’ve got a pair of apps that help you get a little reading done, albeit with two different approaches. Still, whether you prefer having someone else read the articles to you or if you just prefer a cleaner feed of articles, there’s something for everyone in this week’s App Report.</p>
<p>If there’s something you think belongs in our weekly look at promising macOS and iOS apps, just drop us a line at <code>apps@sixcolors.com</code> with a brief description of the app in question. We can’t reply to every email we get, but we’ll certainly take a look at any and all apps for a future column.</p>
<h2>Listen Later</h2>
<p>Six Colors readers may be familiar with <a href="https://www.listenlater.net">Listen Later</a>, which has provided a way to turn articles you’ve been meaning to read into podcasts that you can listen to at your convenience. Recently, there’s been an expansion in the ways you can access the service, with both an <a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/listen-later-text-to-podcast/id6763804820">iOS app</a> and a <a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/listen-later-for-safari/id6769470595?mt=12">Safari extension</a> giving you new ways to send URLs and PDFs to Listen Later so that they can be converted into a podcast.</p>
<p>In the iOS version of Listen Later, a Submit tab offers a field for pasting text, HTML or links; there’s also an optional button for uploading PDFs to the service. I’m able to link Listen Later to my podcatcher of choice — in this case, it’s <a href="https://sixcolors.com/link/2024/07/overcast-turns-10/">Overcast</a> — and a new episode featuring the content I’ve submitted shows up in my feed after it’s been converted to audio form.</p>
<p>The Safari extension for macOS works much the same way, only there’s a button next to the search/URL field that I tap when I want to submit something to Listen Later.</p>
<p>Both the Listen Later iOS app and Safari extension for macOS are free downloads, with $2 in credits for new users. If you like what you hear, you’ll need to purchase additional credits through Listen Later to cover the cost of AI processing and audio generation.</p>
<h2>PageFeed</h2>
<figure class="pull-right"><img data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/pagefeed-merged-6c.png?ssl=1" alt="Two iPhones showing screenshots. Left: article titled “First Look: iOS 27 Public Beta – Six Colors” with text about iOS 27 and Siri AI. Right: PageFeed list including the same article and one about Pixel 11 Pro." data-image-w="" data-image-h="" class=" jetpack-broken-image"><figcaption></figcaption></figure>
<p>Reading articles on your iPhone can be a delight, but scrolling from the start of the article to the end can take some of the pleasure out of the process. If that’s a problem for you, <a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/pagefeed/id6777253589">PageFeed</a> comes to the rescue by adopting a more book-like format for a news reader. Instead of scrolling, you tap to turn the page to make your way through an article.</p>
<p>The idea, PageFeed developer Brad Moss says in the description for the iOS app, is to add some degree of calm to reading through your news feed. You can select the sources and topics you want to read about. A share sheet can also send articles you find elsewhere to PageFeed for a scrolling-free experience.</p>
<p>You can download the app to your iPhone or iPad for free and use it for 14 days to get a feel for PageFeed’s approach. After that, a one-time $10 fee unlocks the app for good.</p>
<h2>Tansei</h2>
<figure class="pull-right"><img data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/tansei3-6c.png?ssl=1" alt="Clipboard app 'Tansei 7' shows items from 'YESTERDAY': Melora Hardin Remembers Playing a 'Ch...', Get Your Phil, Image from Clipboard, ## Tansei, #782635, Image from Clipboard, The New Women’s Pro Baseball League… 3 days left · Activate, Saving what you copy." data-image-w="" data-image-h="" class=" jetpack-broken-image"><figcaption></figcaption></figure>
<p>It would be unfair to describe <a href="https://tansei.io">Tansei</a> as kind of a universal clipboard for your Mac, even if that’s what this utility does, at least in a broad sense. But Tansei is really a storage space for <em>anything</em>. Besides text and URLs, it can hold images, screenshots and even colors, keeping them in a shelf on the side of your display for you to access whenever you need.</p>
<p>In my testing, Tansei has already proven its worth for a <a href="https://get-your-phil-newsletter-ccdfef.beehiiv.com">weekly newsletter</a> I put together every week. I include a lot of links in that newsletter, and previously, that meant keeping an open text document where I could copy down URLs to articles of interest for use when it came time to put a new issue together. With Tansei running on my Mac, all I have to do is copy the URL, and it shows up in Tansei’s shelf automatically. I can also drag-and-drop items into Tansei, and hide the app off to the side when I want an uncluttered screen.</p>
<p>I wish there was a way to exert a little more control over what gets copied. For example, while moving the newsletter text into the publishing site I use, <em>every</em> block of text I copy over appears in Tansei. At least the app offers a bulk management feature that I can use to delete unnecessary items in one fell swoop.</p>
<p>You can buy Tansei for a one-time payment of $7, though a three-day trial lets you use the app for free without restrictions.</p>
<h2>WidgetScreen</h2>
<figure><img data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/widgetscreen2-6c.png?ssl=1" alt="macOS Layout Studio showing widget arrangement: clock 4:35 PM, weather 64°F, calendar Aug 19 over lake scene. Sidebar lists widget types; controls include Add Widget, size, Dark, Current Wallpaper." data-image-w="" data-image-h="" class=" jetpack-broken-image"><figcaption></figcaption></figure>
<p>The release of macOS Sonoma three years ago brought desktop widgets to the Mac. <a href="https://widgetscreen.app">WidgetScreen</a> now pulls off the same trick for your Mac’s lock screen, letting you add weather forecasts, calendars and other bits of information that remain visible even before you’ve unlocked your computer.</p>
<p>Specifically, WidgetScreen includes widgets for weather, clock, stopwatch, reminders and battery. You can also add a Now Playing widget that controls music playback or a Pomodoro widget for when you want to focus or take breaks. A $10 Premium purchase unlocks additional widgets, such as Live Lyrics and News, while giving you greater customization powers over your lock screen.</p>
<figure><img data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/widget-phil-6c.jpg?ssl=1" alt="A laptop on a wooden table showing a lock screen with a clear lake and rocks. Text: Wed Aug 19, 4:37; San Francisco 64° Partly Cloudy; Calendar 19." data-image-w="" data-image-h="" class=" jetpack-broken-image"><figcaption></figcaption></figure>
<p>Not everyone is going to want to bring the widget experience to their Mac’s lock screen, but if you’ve ever wanted to replicate the iPhone’s widget capabilities, WidgetScreen feels like a simple way to do that.</p>
<h2>MailTwin</h2>
<figure><img data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/twinmail2-6c.png?ssl=1" alt="Hi Lisa, sounds good—count me in. I’ll bring something. What clothes should I wear, and what time will the event end?" data-image-w="" data-image-h="" class=" jetpack-broken-image"><figcaption></figcaption></figure>
<p>I’m not fully sold on the value of AI-powered writing tools at this point in time, though that could be the professional writer in me talking. For people who don’t mash sentences together for a living, every little bit of writing assistance helps, especially when it comes to dashing off emails.</p>
<p>So in that sense, I can see the use case for <a href="https://www.mailtwin.ai">MailTwin</a>, which promises to help you draft emails using a voice drawn from the messages you’ve previously sent. Even more helpfully, MailTwin can differentiate between different email accounts if you keep your personal and business correspondence separate, so that the messages it generates aimed to be tonally appropriate depending on context. Replies are generated in a separate window, and you can paste the results into a draft in macOS’s Mail app if you’re satisfied.</p>
<p>MailTwin can tie into eight different AI providers, including the Apple Intelligence that’s already on your Mac. The data only passes between your Mac and the AI service, with nothing ever winding up on a MailTwin server.</p>
<p>You can try out the app for free. After 30 days, you’ll need to pay $20 to unlock the app, though that’s a one-time fee.</p>
